---
import Tool from '../../layouts/Tool.astro';
---
<Tool title="Ad budget & break-even ACoS calculator" description="Set a daily ad budget from target clicks and cost per click, and find the advertising cost of sale (ACoS) at which an ad-driven sale stops making money." lede="Two numbers to know before you raise a budget: what it costs per day, and the ACoS above which each ad-driven sale loses money.">
  <form slot="calc" class="calc" id="pp" onsubmit="return false">
    <div class="calc-in"><h2>Your campaign</h2>
      <div class="field-row"><div class="field"><label for="price">Selling price ($)</label><input id="price" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="25" /></div><div class="field"><label for="profit">Profit before ads ($)</label><input id="profit" type="number" step="0.01" value="7" /></div></div>
      <div class="field-row"><div class="field"><label for="cpc">Cost per click ($)</label><input id="cpc" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="0.9" /></div><div class="field"><label for="cvr">Conversion rate (%)</label><input id="cvr" type="number" min="0" step="0.1" value="10" /></div></div>
      <div class="field"><label for="clicks">Target clicks per day</label><input id="clicks" type="number" min="0" value="40" /></div>
      <button class="btn btn-cta" type="submit">Calculate</button>
    </div>
    <div class="calc-out" aria-live="polite"><h2>Result</h2>
      <div class="out big"><span>Break-even ACoS</span><span class="v" id="o-be">—</span></div>
      <div class="out"><span>Daily budget</span><span class="v" id="o-bud">—</span></div>
      <div class="out"><span>Orders per day</span><span class="v" id="o-ord">—</span></div>
      <div class="out"><span>Expected ACoS</span><span class="v" id="o-acos">—</span></div>
      <div class="out"><span>Profit per day after ads</span><span class="v" id="o-pd">—</span></div>
      <p class="verdict" id="o-v"></p>
    </div>
  </form>
  <h2>Formulas</h2>
  <ul><li>Daily budget = clicks × cost per click.</li><li>Orders = clicks × conversion rate; ad cost per order = cost per click ÷ conversion rate.</li><li>ACoS = ad cost per order ÷ price. Break-even ACoS = profit before ads ÷ price.</li></ul>
  <p>Use cost-per-click and conversion figures from your own reports over enough orders to be stable. Break-even ACoS is a ceiling: a campaign at exactly that level adds sales but no profit.</p>
  <h2>Worked example (hypothetical numbers)</h2>
  <p>Say a product sells for $25.00 and leaves $7.00 of profit after product cost, shipping and platform fees, before any advertising. Break-even ACoS is 7 ÷ 25 = 28%: you can spend up to $7.00 of ads per ad-driven order before that order loses money. If clicks cost $0.90 and one click in ten converts (10%), each ad-driven order costs 0.90 ÷ 0.10 = $9.00 in clicks, an ACoS of 9 ÷ 25 = 36%. That is above the 28% ceiling, so every ad-driven sale loses $2.00. Lifting conversion to 15% cuts the cost per order to $6.00 (24% ACoS) and turns each order into a $1.00 gain; cutting the bid to $0.60 at 10% conversion does the same.</p>
  <h2>How to read the result</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Break-even ACoS</strong> depends only on your price and your profit before ads. Fix those inputs first; a wrong profit figure makes every other output wrong.</li>
    <li><strong>Ad cost per order</strong> is the number to watch week to week. It moves with both bids and conversion, and it can be compared directly with your profit per unit.</li>
    <li><strong>Daily budget</strong> is a cap on spend, not a target. The calculator multiplies the clicks you expect by the cost per click you enter; it does not predict how many clicks a platform will deliver.</li>
    <li>Conversion rates from a handful of orders are noisy. Re-run the numbers when you have more data rather than reacting to a single day.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>What this calculator leaves out</h2>
  <p>It treats every ad-driven order as one unit at full price. Coupons, multi-unit orders, returns and any organic sales that advertising may bring later are not modelled. Platform fees are not looked up here; work out your profit before ads with the <a href="/tools/margin-calculator/">profit and margin calculator</a>, which uses each marketplace's published fees, and bring that figure back to this page.</p>
  <script>
    const v = (id: string) => +(document.getElementById(id) as HTMLInputElement).value; const set = (id: string, t: string) => (document.getElementById(id)!.textContent = t); const $m = (n: number) => (n < 0 ? '−$' : '$') + Math.abs(n).toFixed(2);
    function run() { const P = v('price'), pr = v('profit'), cpc = v('cpc'), cvr = v('cvr') / 100, cl = v('clicks'); const be = P ? pr / P : 0, ord = cl * cvr, cpo = cvr ? cpc / cvr : Infinity, acos = P ? cpo / P : 0;
      set('o-be', P ? (be * 100).toFixed(1) + '%' : '—'); set('o-bud', $m(cl * cpc)); set('o-ord', ord.toFixed(1)); set('o-acos', isFinite(acos) ? (acos * 100).toFixed(1) + '%' : '—'); set('o-pd', isFinite(cpo) ? $m(ord * (pr - cpo)) : '—');
      set('o-v', !isFinite(cpo) ? 'Enter a conversion rate above zero.' : acos > be ? 'At these inputs, ad-driven sales lose money.' : 'At these inputs, ad-driven sales stay profitable.'); }
    document.getElementById('pp')!.addEventListener('input', run); document.getElementById('pp')!.addEventListener('submit', run); run();
  </script>
</Tool>
